1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,907
Good morning. Now, we've been asked to choose an aspect of European clothing or fashion

2
00:00:06,907 --> 00:00:10,360
and to talk about its development over time.

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:16,960
I decided to focus on a rather small area of clothing and that's pockets.

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:20,090
I chose pockets for two reasons, really.

5
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:22,593
We all have them - in jeans,

6
00:00:22,593 --> 00:00:25,700
jackets, coats, for example - and even

7
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,851
though we often carry bags or briefcases as well,

8
00:00:28,851 --> 00:00:36,360
nothing is quite as convenient as being able to pop your phone or credit card into your pocket.

9
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,213
Yet, I suspect that, other than that,

10
00:00:39,213 --> 00:00:42,680
people don't really think about pockets too much

11
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:46,550
and they're rather overlooked as a fashion item.

12
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:50,200
It's certainly very interesting to go back in time

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,910
and see how pockets developed for men and women.

14
00:00:53,910 --> 00:01:01,270
In the 18th century, fashions were quite different from the way they are now, and pockets were too.

15
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:04,640
If we think about male fashion first

16
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:09,220
that was the time when suits became popular.

17
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:13,699
Trousers were knee-length only and referred to as 'breeches',

18
00:01:13,699 --> 00:01:18,660
the waistcoats were short and the jackets were long

19
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:21,165
but all three garments were lined

20
00:01:21,165 --> 00:01:28,630
with material and pockets were sewn into this cloth by whichever tailor the customer used.

21
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:34,842
The wearer could then carry small objects such as pencils or coins on their person

22
00:01:34,842 --> 00:01:38,570
and reach them through a gap in the lining.

23
00:01:38,570 --> 00:01:43,680
Coat pockets became increasingly decorative on the outside for men

24
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,738
who wanted to look stylish,

25
00:01:45,738 --> 00:01:51,472
but they were often larger but plainer if the wearer was someone

26
00:01:51,472 --> 00:01:53,840
with a profession who needed to carry medical instruments

27
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:58,640
a doctor or physician, for example.

28
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:04,280
The development of women's pockets was a little different.

29
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:10,480
For one thing, they weren't nearly as visible or as easy to reach as men's.

30
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:13,162
In the 18th and 19th centuries,

31
00:02:13,162 --> 00:02:16,760
women carried numerous possessions on their person

32
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:21,210
and some of these could be worth a lot of money.

33
00:02:21,210 --> 00:02:24,020
Women were more vulnerable to theft

34
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:29,870
and wealthy women, in particular, worried constantly about pickpockets.

35
00:02:29,870 --> 00:02:36,800
So - what they did was to have a pair of pockets made that were tied together with string.

36
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:39,682
The pockets were made of fabric,

37
00:02:39,682 --> 00:02:41,380
which might be recycled cloth

38
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:46,887
if the wearer had little money or something more expensive,

39
00:02:46,887 --> 00:02:50,730
such as linen, sometimes featuring very delicate embroidery.

40
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:56,930
Women tied the pockets around their waist so that they hung beneath their clothes.

41
00:02:56,930 --> 00:02:58,818
Remember, skirts were long then

42
00:02:58,818 --> 00:03:07,500
and there was plenty of room to hide a whole range of small possessions between the layers of petticoats that were commonly worn.

43
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:12,153
They would have an opening in the folds of their skirts through

44
00:03:12,153 --> 00:03:16,030
which they could reach whatever they needed, like their perfume.

45
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:26,016
Working women, of course, also needed to carry around items that they might use for whatever job or trade they were involved in,

46
00:03:26,016 --> 00:03:31,660
but their pairs of pockets still remained on the inside of their clothing,

47
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:36,870
they just got bigger or longer - sometimes reaching down to their knees!

48
00:03:36,870 --> 00:03:41,834
So the tie-on pockets went well into the 19th century

49
00:03:41,834 --> 00:03:47,790
and only changed when fashion altered towards the end of that period.

50
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:51,700
That's when dresses became tighter and less bulky

51
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:55,041
and the pairs of pockets became very noticeable -

52
00:03:55,041 --> 00:04:00,160
they stood out too much and detracted from the woman's image.

53
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:05,635
Women who had been used to carrying around a range of personal possessions -

54
00:04:05,635 --> 00:04:11,110
and still wanted to - needed somewhere to carry these items about their person.

55
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:16,633
That was when small bags, or pouches as they were known,

56
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:18,980
came into fashion and, of course

57
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:25,168
they inevitably led on to the handbag of more modern times,

58
00:04:25,168 --> 00:04:28,180
particularly when fashion removed pockets altogether.
